th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Probability-Theory-for-EOR/monotonicity-of-expectation.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Probability-Theory-for-EOR/monotonicity-of-expectation.html","questions":[{"id":"pt_batch8_monotonicity_of_expectation_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does monotonicity of expectation state?","options":["If $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then $E[X]\\le E[Y]$","If $X\\le Y$, then $E[X]=E[Y]$ always","If $E[X]\\le E[Y]$, then $X\\le Y$ always","If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $X\\le Y$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b6b0fd1546620fb805cea0cc","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch8_monotonicity_of_expectation_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What condition on expectations is required in monotonicity of expectation?","options":["They are finite","They are equal","They are both zero","They are both variances"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d58b26425e58ed557edf6eb7","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch8_monotonicity_of_expectation_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the phrase 'almost surely' mean in monotonicity of expectation's condition?","options":["The ordering must fail roughly half of the time","It may fail only on a probability-zero event","It concerns only the expected values","The variables must be deterministic ones"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e69fcff1838a69381d6c06f5","time_limit":25},{"id":"pt_batch8_monotonicity_of_expectation_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $0\\le X\\le 5$ almost surely, what bound follows for $E[X]$?","options":["$E[X]\\ge5$","$0\\le E[X]\\le 5$","$E[X]\\le0$","$E[X]=5$ always"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7b0d08fea13caa9fd06155ce","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch8_monotonicity_of_expectation_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $X\\le 2Y$ almost surely, $E[X]$ and $E[Y]$ exist, and $E[Y]=3$, what bound follows?","options":["$E[X]\\ge 6$","$E[X]=3$","$E[X]\\le 3$","$E[X]\\le 6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"73d36abafbecdab4401cda71","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch8_monotonicity_of_expectation_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ equal $-1$ or $1$ with probability $1/2$ each, and let $Y=0$. Since $E[X]=E[Y]=0$, what does this example show?","options":["Equal expectations need not give an almost-sure comparison","$X\\le Y$ almost surely because its expectation is no larger","$Y\\le X$ almost surely because their expectations are equal","$X$ and $Y$ must have the same distribution because their means agree"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fcfb968453c222516257647d","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch8_monotonicity_of_expectation_013_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X$ have finite expectation, let $c$ be a real number, and cap $X$ at $c$ by $Z=\\min(X,c)$. Which statement holds for every such $X$ and $c$?","options":["$\\mathbb{E}Z<\\mathbb{E}X$ strictly, since high values are cut off","$\\mathbb{E}Z=\\min(\\mathbb{E}X,c)$, since the cap passes through $\\mathbb{E}$","$\\mathbb{E}Z\\le\\mathbb{E}X$, since $Z\\le X$ for every outcome","No comparison follows unless $X\\le c$ almost surely"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0878af2dad45d630973c9590","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch8_monotonicity_of_expectation_019_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X\\ge0$ with $\\mathbb{E}X=2$. Since $10\\,\\mathbf{1}_{\\{X\\ge10\\}}\\le X$, which bound follows from monotonicity?","options":["$\\mathbb{P}(X\\ge10)\\ge0.2$","$\\mathbb{P}(X\\ge10)=0.2$","$\\mathbb{P}(X\\ge10)\\le0.02$","$\\mathbb{P}(X\\ge10)\\le0.2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1b7d092632423543d43e3e1a","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch8_monotonicity_of_expectation_020_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ and $Y$ have finite expectations with $X\\le Y$ almost surely. Which condition is necessary and sufficient for $\\mathbb{E}X<\\mathbb{E}Y$?","options":["$X<Y$ for at least one outcome","$\\mathbb{P}(X<Y)>0$","$X\\le Y$ for every outcome, not just almost surely","$X$ and $Y$ are independent"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"82119a528158d02ba96edeab","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_probability_theory_for_eor_monotonicity_of_expectation_c79b90b0_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Random variables $X$ and $Y$ have finite expectations and satisfy $\\mathbb{P}(X\\le Y)=0.99$. What can be concluded about $\\mathbb{E}X$ and $\\mathbb{E}Y$?","options":["$\\mathbb{E}X\\le\\mathbb{E}Y$, since the order fails only with probability $0.01$.","$\\mathbb{E}X\\le\\mathbb{E}Y$ holds with probability $0.99$.","Neither order of $\\mathbb{E}X$ and $\\mathbb{E}Y$ is guaranteed.","$\\mathbb{E}X>\\mathbb{E}Y$, since the order does not hold almost surely."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"65b59d5101ac3d9a1c355ce9","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1b_probability_theory_for_eor_monotonicity_of_expectation_c79b90b0_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A random variable $X$ has finite expectation and $\\mathbb{E}|X|=3$. What is the set of all possible values of $\\mathbb{E}X$?","options":["$\\{-3,3\\}$","$[0,3]$","$(-\\infty,-3]\\cup[3,\\infty)$","$[-3,3]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a8fd404db76195749c9738ee","time_limit":30}]}}